-- Phase 7 Task 6. Nothing here is generated by Prisma and nothing here can be: Prisma models no
-- CHECK constraints, so this whole migration is hand-written, and `prisma migrate diff` against a
-- database that already has this constraint emits "This is an empty migration" — measured on a
-- throwaway `badam_p7t6_probe`, the same probe Phase 4 Task 2 wished it had run for an index.
--
-- `Space.key` has been a bare `String @unique` with no validation anywhere since Phase 1. The
-- reasoning for the constraint, for what `src/lib/spaces/key.ts` still owes on top of it, and for
-- what a key created *before* it can still do, is on that module and on the `Space` model in
-- `prisma/schema.prisma`.
--
-- The pattern is `System_key_shape`'s, character for character, and the tie is deliberate: a space
-- key and a system key are the same thing measured the same way — one lowercase URL segment with
-- both ends alphanumeric. `src/lib/pages/slug.ts`'s `validateUrlSegment` is the single application
-- statement of it that both `validateSpaceKey` and `validateSystemKey` call. As there, the
-- trailing `-` inside the bracket expression is a literal and is why it is last, and there is
-- deliberately **no length bound**: retyping `SLUG_MAX_LENGTH` as a number in SQL that nothing ties
-- back to `src/lib/pages/slug.ts` is a predicate stated twice with one copy untestable.
--
-- ## This is the folded-uniqueness constraint, and there is deliberately no second index
--
-- `Space_key_key` already exists. On a column no row of which can hold an uppercase character,
-- `key = lower(key)` for every row, so that unique index **is** a unique index on `lower(key)` —
-- Task 1's insight for `System`, arriving where it was actually needed. A
-- `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ON "Space" (lower(key))` would be a second statement of one guarantee, and
-- the one whose removal no test could see. The class is ASCII, so the Unicode half closes with it:
-- `src/lib/git/paths.ts` measured that this machine's filesystem is case-insensitive and would give
-- `Engineering` and `engineering` one directory, and declined to fold there because "a collision is
-- a property of two space keys and this function sees one". This is the point where both are
-- visible.
--
-- ## Unlike `System`, this table is POPULATED, and the ALTER was verified before it was written
--
-- A CHECK against a populated table fails outright if any row violates it, and a failed migration
-- mid-deploy is worse than the debt. `System` got its CHECK in the migration that created an empty
-- table and had nothing to verify. Here the two rows — `engineering` and `handbook` — were checked
-- against this exact pattern in SQL first, and the failure mode was reproduced on the probe rather
-- than imagined: with a third row keyed `eng ops` present, this statement is
--
--     ERROR: check constraint "Space_key_shape" of relation "Space" is violated by some row
--
-- No such row exists in the development database. Two **test fixtures** did hold one, and the
-- seeded rows said nothing about them — see this task's closing note.
--
-- ## What this does not do
--
-- It does not rewrite anything. No key is folded, trimmed or renamed: a `Space.key` is the first
-- segment of every URL in the space, the directory name under `content/` in the git mirror, and in
-- every link anyone has shared, so a repair pass would break all three silently. And it does not
-- know about routes — `/admin`, `/systems` and the five other top-level segments are refused by
-- `validateSpaceKey`, because a route list changes when a directory is added under `src/app` and a
-- copy of it in SQL would be the untestable half of a rule stated twice.
ALTER TABLE "Space"
  ADD CONSTRAINT "Space_key_shape"
  CHECK ("key" ~ '^[a-z0-9]([a-z0-9-]*[a-z0-9])?$');
